{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 11863 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e608a289697ac4feb132826a452530f0c772dc8a736455c53e21b5df5fc8fffd" :edb/db-before-t 11862 :edb/db-after-t 11863 :edb/tx-data [[13194139545175 50 #inst "2026-10-30T22:36:54.334Z" 13194139545175 17592186576411 true] [17592186096495 1074 "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite $\\mathbf c$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b,$ we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in $△ O D C$, we have:\n\n- $D C = 2 | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{D C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{D C} = 2 \\mathbf{a} .$\n\nAdditionally,\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O D} + \\overrightarrow{D C} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + 2 \\mathbf{a} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} = 2 \\mathbf{b} + 2 \\mathbf{a} .$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get:\n$$\n\\lambda=2\n$$" 13194139545175 17592186576411 true] [17592186096495 1074 "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite $\\mathbf c$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b,$ we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in $△ O D C$, we have:\n\n- $D C = 2 | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to -}{D C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to -}{D C} = 2 \\mathbf{a} .$\n\nAdditionally,\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overset{\\to -}{O D} + \\overset{\\to -}{D C} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + 2 \\mathbf{a} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} = 2 \\mathbf{b} + 2 \\mathbf{a} .$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get:\n$$\n\\lambda=2\n$$" 13194139545175 17592186576411 false] [17592186097837 1064 "Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted $O$), and another point $X,$ the vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ is called the **position vector** of the point $X.$\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{A X} = \\mathbf{b} .$ How can we find the position vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf{b}?$\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from $O$ to $X$ by first moving from $O$ to $A,$ and then from $A$ to $X.$ Therefore,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{A X} = \\mathbf{b} ,$ we get\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O X} = \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." 13194139545175 17592186576411 true] [17592186097837 1064 "Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted $O$), and another point $X,$ the vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ is called the **position vector** of the point $X.$\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to -}{A X} = \\mathbf{b} .$ How can we find the position vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf{b}?$\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from $O$ to $X$ by first moving from $O$ to $A,$ and then from $A$ to $X.$ Therefore,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to -}{A X} = \\mathbf{b} ,$ we get\n$$\n\\overset{\\to -}{O X} = \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." 13194139545175 17592186576411 false] [17592186119639 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P\\left(-1,1,0\\right)$ and $Q\\left(5,-1, 0\\right).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ which is given by\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\overrightarrow{P Q} &  = \\langle  5 , - 1 , 0 \\rangle  - \\langle  - 1 , 1 , 0 \\rangle  \\\\  &  = \\langle  6 , - 2 , 0 \\rangle  .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-(-1)}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{z-0}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\nor equivalently\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{z}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\dfrac{z}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value $z=0.$ For example, our two points $P\\left(-1,1,0\\right)$ and $Q\\left(5,-1,0\\right)$ both have $z$-coordinates of $z=0.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{z}{0}$ part and instead write $z=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2},\\; z=0 .\n$$" 13194139545175 17592186576411 true] [17592186119639 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P\\left(-1,1,0\\right)$ and $Q\\left(5,-1, 0\\right).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ which is given by\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\overset{\\to -}{P Q} &  = \\langle  5 , - 1 , 0 \\rangle  - \\langle  - 1 , 1 , 0 \\rangle  \\\\  &  = \\langle  6 , - 2 , 0 \\rangle  .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-(-1)}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{z-0}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\nor equivalently\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{z}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\dfrac{z}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value $z=0.$ For example, our two points $P\\left(-1,1,0\\right)$ and $Q\\left(5,-1,0\\right)$ both have $z$-coordinates of $z=0.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{z}{0}$ part and instead write $z=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2},\\; z=0 .\n$$" 13194139545175 17592186576411 false] [17592186234976 1074 "To find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} .\n$$\n\nIn $△ O P C ,$ we have:\n\n- $O P = 2 | \\mathbf{b} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ and $\\mathbf b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O P} = - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- $P C = | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{P C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{P C} = - \\mathbf{a} .\n$$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} \\\\  &  = - 2 \\mathbf{b} + ( - \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\  &  = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-1.\n$$" 13194139545175 17592186576411 true] [17592186234976 1074 "To find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\overset{\\to -}{O P} + \\overset{\\to -}{P C} .\n$$\n\nIn $△ O P C ,$ we have:\n\n- $O P = 2 | \\mathbf{b} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ and $\\mathbf b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overset{\\to -}{O P} = - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- $P C = | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to -}{P C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overset{\\to -}{P C} = - \\mathbf{a} .\n$$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overset{\\to -}{O P} + \\overset{\\to -}{P C} \\\\  &  = - 2 \\mathbf{b} + ( - \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\  &  = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-1.\n$$" 13194139545175 17592186576411 false] [17592186362403 1064 "We can still find a position vector by tracing a path from the origin to the point and adding the vectors along that path.\n\nHowever, if one part of the path points in the opposite direction to a known vector, then we write it as a **negative** multiple of that vector.\n\nFor example, consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d5/d5366a68ed98e7f614ff998f69098613ea8e12d05bc27274ea66e8ce5a908cb7.png)\n\nWe are given that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b}$ and we are asked to find $\\lambda.$\n\nTo find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nLet $O$ be the origin, and let $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf c$ end at points $A$ and $C,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\nSince one term in the vector sum is $2 \\mathbf{b} ,$ we first draw the vector $2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc976b12b4610c69953545d95662bf5535ef2a92eb9d9cbd63e087c85f3b98e7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2 \\mathbf{b} .$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} .\n$$\n\nFrom the construction, we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O P} = 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nAlso, the vector $\\overrightarrow{P C}$ has length $2 | \\mathbf{a} | ,$ but points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf a.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{P C} = - 2 \\mathbf{a} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + ( - 2 \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\  &  = - 2 \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} = - 2 \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-2.\n$$" 13194139545175 17592186576411 true] [17592186362403 1064 "We can still find a position vector by tracing a path from the origin to the point and adding the vectors along that path.\n\nHowever, if one part of the path points in the opposite direction to a known vector, then we write it as a **negative** multiple of that vector.\n\nFor example, consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d5/d5366a68ed98e7f614ff998f69098613ea8e12d05bc27274ea66e8ce5a908cb7.png)\n\nWe are given that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b}$ and we are asked to find $\\lambda.$\n\nTo find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nLet $O$ be the origin, and let $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf c$ end at points $A$ and $C,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\nSince one term in the vector sum is $2 \\mathbf{b} ,$ we first draw the vector $2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc976b12b4610c69953545d95662bf5535ef2a92eb9d9cbd63e087c85f3b98e7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2 \\mathbf{b} .$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\overset{\\to -}{O P} + \\overset{\\to -}{P C} .\n$$\n\nFrom the construction, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to -}{O P} = 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nAlso, the vector $\\overset{\\to -}{P C}$ has length $2 | \\mathbf{a} | ,$ but points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf a.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to -}{P C} = - 2 \\mathbf{a} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overset{\\to -}{O P} + \\overset{\\to -}{P C} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + ( - 2 \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\  &  = - 2 \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} = - 2 \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-2.\n$$" 13194139545175 17592186576411 false] [17592186362404 1064 "Now let's look at a slightly more complicated diagram.\n\nSuppose we have two vectors $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and a parallelogram $OAXB_1,$ where $\\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} = 2 \\overrightarrow{O B} .$ How can we find the position vector $\\mathbf{x} = \\overrightarrow{O X}$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf{b}?$\n\n![](images/c7/c78c8895790b28c7dbe60d0dd31a5375fef19ba2d6fa668144859e6fafaf33bb.png)\n\nAs before, we trace a path from the origin to the point $X.$ From the diagram, we can write\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{{B}_{1} X} .\n$$\n\nWe know that\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} = 2 \\overrightarrow{O B} = 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nAlso, since $OAXB_1$ is a parallelogram, opposite sides are equal and parallel. So,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{B_1X}=\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for $\\mathbf x,$ we get\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{x} &  = \\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{{B}_{1} X} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{a} \\\\  &  = \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, even when the path uses translated copies of known vectors, we can still find the position vector by expressing each part of the path in terms of the known vectors and then adding.\n\nNow we will use the same path-tracing idea in a slightly different way. Instead of only writing a position vector, we will also use vector paths to identify unknown coefficients in linear combinations of known vectors." 13194139545175 17592186576411 true] [17592186362404 1064 "Now let's look at a slightly more complicated diagram.\n\nSuppose we have two vectors $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and a parallelogram $OAXB_1,$ where $\\overset{\\to -}{O {B}_{1}} = 2 \\overset{\\to -}{O B} .$ How can we find the position vector $\\mathbf{x} = \\overset{\\to -}{O X}$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf{b}?$\n\n![](images/c7/c78c8895790b28c7dbe60d0dd31a5375fef19ba2d6fa668144859e6fafaf33bb.png)\n\nAs before, we trace a path from the origin to the point $X.$ From the diagram, we can write\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\overset{\\to -}{O {B}_{1}} + \\overset{\\to -}{{B}_{1} X} .\n$$\n\nWe know that\n$$\n\\overset{\\to -}{O {B}_{1}} = 2 \\overset{\\to -}{O B} = 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nAlso, since $OAXB_1$ is a parallelogram, opposite sides are equal and parallel. So,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{B_1X}=\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for $\\mathbf x,$ we get\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{x} &  = \\overset{\\to -}{O {B}_{1}} + \\overset{\\to -}{{B}_{1} X} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{a} \\\\  &  = \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, even when the path uses translated copies of known vectors, we can still find the position vector by expressing each part of the path in terms of the known vectors and then adding.\n\nNow we will use the same path-tracing idea in a slightly different way. Instead of only writing a position vector, we will also use vector paths to identify unknown coefficients in linear combinations of known vectors." 13194139545175 17592186576411 false]] :edb/tempids {}}
